  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nMid level ridging along the East Coast is looking less sturdy \nfor today than guidance has portrayed over the past few forecast\ncycles. Short wave over Upstate NY will cross after day break\nand will likely maintain mostly cloudy skies. Latest water vapor\nimagery shows plenty of moisture traversing east across the \nGreat Lakes region while much of model guidance suggests there \nwill be at least partly sunny skies in the wake of the short \nwave. Cloud cover is then forecast to increase this afternoon as\nthe moisture from the Great Lakes moves overhead. Overall, \nstill looking at a warm day with temperatures hanging closer to \nthe mid to low 70s. A few light showers cannot be ruled out \nacross NH this afternoon while today will likely be mostly dry. \nSouthwest flow tonight will aid in keeping temperatures in the \n60s for lows and limit the potential for fog.\n\nA cold front will approach from the NW Friday and cross during\nthe afternoon and evening. A warm airmass will be in place ahead\nof the front and temperatures climbing into the 70s shouldn't be\na problem. However, guidance has trended faster with the FROPA\nand increasing clouds have resulted in the downward trends in\nhighs on Friday. The front will bring chances for showers and\nperhaps some instances of thunder across the area from late\nmorning into the afternoon. Rainfall amounts are unimpressive\nwith much of the area averaging less than a tenth of an inch\nwhile some locally higher amounts are possible in any\nconvection.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nSeasonably strong cold air advection behind Friday night's front as \n850 mb temps nosedive to 2-5 C by Saturday evening, which will be \nquite an airmass change. Even more impressive is dew points falling \nfrom the 60s Friday to 30s on Saturday, in some locations a 30 \ndegree drop within 18-24 hours! Highs Saturday will still reach the \n60s with plenty of sunshine, but it will certainly be a very \ndifferent airmass from Friday with a true crisp fall feel!\n\nVery strong agreement on global guidance in another dynamic trough \nand attendant cold front approaching the region late Sunday then \ncrossing through Monday. Similar to the first wave, a bit of a \nglancing blow with the surface low and attendant vort max well to \nthe north up in Quebec. Guidance is currently split as ECMWF members \nshow some southern wave energy clipping the southern half of the \nforecast area Sunday night, as well as some precipitation again \nMonday during the day in northern zones close to the best forcing \nalong the front. GFS solutions meanwhile show these features \nsplitting the region with a mostly dry frontal passage. For now the \nrainfall potential is still up in the air, but even with wetter \nsolutions, totals are not likely to be impressive given a \nprogressive setup combined with a lack of deep moisture. Most likely \nimpacts will be increasing cloud cover Sunday and evening rain \nchances, with clearing conditions Monday afternoon. Both days should \nfeature highs near 60 to mid 60s, but Monday may be cooler in the \nhigher elevations as well as potentially breezy behind the front.\n\nVery likely the coldest weather since the spring arrives Tuesday \nonward with 850 mb temperatures plunging to between 0 and -7C by \nTuesday morning. Even 925 mb temperatures are near or slightly below \nfreezing on guidance. If the boundary layer can successfully \ndecouple with winds slacking, it is likely a significant chunk of \nthe forecast area could see the first hard freeze of the season \nTuesday and/or Wednesday. Even with full mixing/sunshine, highs may \nstruggle to reach 60 Tuesday-Wednesday given the very chilly air \naloft.",
